    "text": "In Rust, `Vec<T>` and `&[T]` (a slice) are both used to represent collections, but they have different ownership semantics, mutability, and capabilities!\n\n**Vec<T>**:\n- A `Vec` is an **owned**, growable array on the heap.\n- It owns its contents and has a concrete length (similar to an Array) but can be dynamically resized.\n- You can pass a `Vec` by value (moving ownership) or by reference (`&[T]` or `&mut [T]`).\n\n**[T] (Slice)**:\n- A slice is a **view** into a contiguous sequence of elements. It can be created from a `Vec`, an array, or a string.\n- A slice doesn't own its data; it just points to it.\n- Slices can be immutable (`&[T]`) or mutable (`&mut [T]`).\n- A slice has a concrete length and can be used to efficiently iterate over elements without needing to move the entire `Vec`.\n\n**Examples**:\n\n```rust\nlet vec = vec![1, 2, 3];\n\n// Vec owns its data\nprintln!(\"vec: {:?}\", vec);\n\n// &Vec (slice) is a reference to the Vec\nlet slice: &[i32] = &vec;\nprintln!(\"slice: {:?}\", slice);\n\n// Mutable slice\nlet mut_slice: &mut [i32] = &mut vec;\n*mut_slice[0] = 10;\n```\n\n**Use Cases**:\n- Use **`Vec`** when you need to own the data and grow/shrink it.\n- Use **`slice`** when you want to pass data by reference, iterate over collections, or work with sub-collections without moving ownership.\n\n**Memory Layout**:\n- `Vec<T>`: Stores its data on the heap.\n- `&[T]` (slice): Stores a pointer to the data and a length.\n\n**Implementation**:\n\n```rust\nstruct Vec<T> {\n    len: usize,\n    capacity: usize,\n    data: *mut T,\n}\n\nstruct Slice<'a, T> {\n    pointer: *const T,\n    len: usize,\n}\n```\n\nSlices are used for borrowing, sub-segments, and efficient iteration.\n`Vec` is for ownership and dynamic memory management.\n\nI hope this clarifies the difference!\n",
